266: with \(R_0(\phi)Q=Qe^{-i\phi}\). Thus the written \(H\)-axis and
267: (OP.18) are reached from the full-unit action by the displayed based
268: logarithm contraction, with the real normal metric transported along
269: that contraction. Together with their base rotations, (OP.19) gives
270: the actual transported circle actions on all three tubes.
271: They agree with the vertex action on the protected collars.
272: 
273: The product in OP.3 thus provides a possible ordinary common boundary
274: marking, with the nonconstant circle action (OP.12)--(OP.18). The
275: circle-trace and stable-derivative arguments for a constant equivariant
276: product require a further correction in this marking. That correction
277: is not evaluated here. In particular neither a rank-one initial
278: variation matrix nor vanishing nonfixed relative derivatives follows
279: from (OP.17).
280: 
281: ## OP.5. The closed fixed-curve frame comparison
282: 
283: We also compute the actual normal-frame comparison on the fixed
284: curve. Write \(z=\tan\chi\,e^{i\psi}\), with
285: \(0\leq\chi\leq\pi/2\), and put
286: \[
287:  U(z)=\frac{1+jz}{\sqrt{1+|z|^2}},\qquad q_H=U(z)Q.
288: \]
289: The ordinary product of OP.2 and the physical Cox product of P3K.42
290: are therefore related by the literal arrow
291: \[
292:  q_H\longmapsto L(z,\theta)q_H R(z,\theta),\qquad
293:  L=UHU^{-1},\quad R=H^{-1}.                         \tag{OP.20}
294: \]
295: Here the radial parameter in \(H\) is the previously chosen smooth
296: function of \(|z|\). Both maps close on \(\mathbb P^1\times S^1\).
297: At the first pole \(H=1\). Near the second pole \(H=e^{-3i\theta}\),
298: so \(L=\cos3\theta-(UiU^{-1})\sin3\theta\) extends in the smooth
299: Hopf axis; its limiting value is \(e^{3i\theta}\). The right factor
300: extends independently of the source angle. The actual zero-winding
301: diagonal unit maps in OP.19 are carried in this comparison and then
302: contracted by their displayed based logarithms. This is a homotopy
303: of the actual closed frame arrow, rather than a discarded unit.
304: 
305: We orient the domain by its complex curve orientation followed by
306: positive \(\theta\), hence by \(d\chi\wedge d\psi\wedge d\theta\)
307: on the cylinder. In the quaternion orientation \((1,i,j,k)\), use
308: the matrix trace with \(\operatorname{tr}(ijk)=-2\), and the positive
309: normalized form
310: \[
311:  \omega(g)=-\frac{\operatorname{tr}(g^{-1}dg)^3}{24\pi^2}.
312: \]
313: The Maurer--Cartan equations give the exact product identity
314: \[
315:  \omega(gh)=\omega(g)+\omega(h)+
316:  \frac{1}{8\pi^2}d\operatorname{tr}
317:        (g^{-1}dg\wedge dh\,h^{-1}).                  \tag{OP.21}
318: \]
319: Applying it twice to \(UHU^{-1}\), the two \(U\) cubic terms cancel
320: and the \(H\) term vanishes because \(H\) depends on only two
321: variables. Thus its integral is the boundary integral of
322: \[
323:  \frac{1}{8\pi^2}\left\{
324:  \operatorname{tr}(U^{-1}dU\wedge dH\,H^{-1})+
325:  \operatorname{tr}((UH)^{-1}d(UH)\wedge dU^{-1}U)\right\}.
326:                                                                \tag{OP.22}
327: \]
328: There is no first-pole contribution. At the second pole,
329: \(U=je^{i\psi}\), \(H=e^{-3i\theta}\), and
330: \[
331:  U^{-1}dU=i\,d\psi,\qquad dH\,H^{-1}=-3i\,d\theta.
332: \]
333: Each trace in braces in (OP.22) is
334: \(6\,d\psi\wedge d\theta\). The outward boundary orientation there
335: is \(d\psi\wedge d\theta\), and consequently
336: \[
337:  \deg L=\frac{12(2\pi)^2}{8\pi^2}=6,\qquad
338:  \deg R=0.                                            \tag{OP.23}
339: \]
340: The second equality follows directly from its two-variable
341: factorization. The Hopf coordinate \(h=UiU^{-1}\) reverses the
342: complex orientation of the source curve: near \(z=0\), its two
343: derivatives in \((\Re z,\Im z)\) are \((-2k,-2j)\), negatively
344: oriented at \(i\). Therefore the same closed comparison has
345: factor degrees \((-6,0)\) in the \(h\)-sphere-times-parameter
346: orientation of P3K.23, and \((6,0)\) in complex-curve orientation.
347: We have computed these closed factor maps and their degrees. The
348: connecting-homomorphism sign and the resulting relative-return
349: correction remain unevaluated; no additional fan return follows
350: without those arrows.
351: 
352: The exact normal-matrix, quaternion and degree checks are in
353: code/part3/source_cusp_ordinary_product.py.
